fact WorkItem(id: "WI-849", description: some(value: "Re-type the operation type-param table from `Vec<(Symbol, TermId)>` to `Vec<(Symbol, Var)>` — the entries ARE variables, and the TermId spelling forces a destructure-and-silently-drop at every reader.\n\nWHAT THE FIELD HOLDS (measured at HEAD b0411496, 2026-07-28). ONE producer: `load_operation` (kb/load.rs:13387-13401) mints each entry as `kb.alloc(Term::Var(Var::Global(vid)))` over `vid = kb.fresh_var(tp_sym)`, `build_list`s them into a ground cons-list and stores it as `Value::term(type_params_list)` (load.rs:13655). `type_params` is deliberately NOT part of the value-fact test (`all_ground`, load.rs:13644, is `params_all_ground && effects_all_ground && matches!(return_value, Value::Term{..})`), so even on a value-fact `OperationInfo` head the field rides as a ground `Value::Term`. `extract_type_params` (kb/op_info.rs:423) decodes it to `Vec<(Symbol, TermId)>` where the Symbol is `vid.name()` and the TermId is the `Term::Var(Global)` itself. So BOTH components of every pair are functions of one VarId, and the type says \"some term\" where the value is always a variable.\n\nWHY TermId IS THE WRONG TYPE — every reader immediately undoes the wrapping, and three of them SILENTLY DROP what doesn't fit:\n  * `extract_type_params` (op_info.rs:430-433) — a `filter_map` that DROPS any element that is not `Term::Var(Global)`.\n  * `rigidify_op_type_params` (kb/typing.rs:33038) — `if let Term::Var(Var::Global(vid)) = kb.get_term(*var_tid)`, then `bind_term(kb, vid, rigid)`; a non-Var entry is silently skipped.\n  * `sort_type_params_as_pairs` (typing.rs:23400-23405) — the same gate as a `.then_some(...)` filter (\"a param whose alias is not a plain `Global` var is skipped\", per its own doc).\n  * `check_unconstrained_type_params` (typing.rs:13182) and `check_signature_self_contradiction` (typing.rs:19819-19821) — re-wrap the TermId as `TermIdView(*var_term)` to get a carrier-neutral read back out.\n  * `walk_term_to_resolved` (typing.rs:24394-24397) — re-destructures `Term::Var(Global)` to reach `subst.resolve_as_value(vid)`, which is what the walk actually wanted.\nThe substrate ALREADY treats a term-var and a value-var as one thing: `resolved_var` (typing.rs:24480-24486) accepts BOTH `Value::Term{id}` → `Term::Var(Global)` AND `Value::Var(Var::Global(vid))`; `walk_value_to_resolved` has a `Value::Var(Global)` arm (typing.rs:24419-24428); `Value` implements `TermView` with a WI-109 Var arm (kb/term_view.rs:1629); typing.rs:26743 bridges `Value::Var(v) -> kb.alloc(Term::Var(*v))`. Nothing in the machinery needs the var to arrive as a TermId.\n\nWHY BARE `Var` AND NOT `Value` / `ViewItem`. `Value::Var(Var)` is a wrapper over the identical payload that re-admits carriers (Node/Entity/Term) which cannot occur here — it would buy a dead arm at every reader, and CLAUDE.md's \"loud error over silent skip\" then makes each of those an `unreachable!`. `ViewItem<'a>` is out on lifetimes regardless of design: it borrows `kb` (op_info.rs:314) and this record is cloned out of the `OpSignature` cache and then read while `kb` is mutably borrowed (`check_unconstrained_type_params(kb: &mut KnowledgeBase, ..., op: &OperationInfoFull, ...)`). `Var` is `Copy` with a manual `PartialEq`/`Eq` (kb/term.rs:82, 166, 177), so the pair stays as cheap as `(Symbol, TermId)` and identity tests still compile.\n\nKEEP THE PAIR — do NOT collapse to `Vec<VarId>`. On the OP side `Symbol == vid.name()` by construction, so the Symbol looks redundant; it is not, because `rigidify_op_type_params` consumes the op table CONCATENATED with `sort_type_params_as_pairs` (typing.rs:33204 `all_params.extend(parent_sort_params.iter().cloned())`), and on the SORT side the Symbol is the QUALIFIED param symbol while the var may be an ANONYMOUS `?` — the rigidify naming comment (typing.rs:33045) exists precisely because `vid.name()` there renders every skolemized param as `?_`. Use `Var` rather than `VarId` for the same reason: it keeps the sort side (which gates on `Var::Global`) expressible unchanged.\n\nKEEP THE KB FACT A HASH-CONSED TERM LIST. The `OperationInfo.type_params` field is persistent asserted structure — exactly CLAUDE.md's hash-consing sweet spot. This ticket changes the DECODED record type and its readers, not the fact representation. `extract_type_params` becomes a total map whose non-`Term::Var(Global)` case is a LOUD error instead of a filter_map drop.\n\nREACHABILITY OF THAT CASE IS UNMEASURED — say so, do not overclaim. The loader is the only in-tree producer and cannot emit a non-var, so the loud arm guards FUTURE producers (and a hand-written `fact anthill.reflect.OperationInfo(...)`, which I have not driven). What it would cost if ever reached is concrete, which is why loud is right: a dropped op type param is invisible in four ways at once — `check_unconstrained_type_params` (typing.rs:13170) never checks it; `resolve_call_type_arg_targets` (typing.rs:13060) cannot find its label, so `op[T = ...]` reports \"no such type parameter\" naming the USER's call for a malformed DECLARATION (the WI-839 failure shape); `rigidify_op_type_params` never skolemizes it, so the body sees a solvable flex var instead of a rigid (WI-392); and `seed_op_type_args` has nothing to match.\n\nONE READER HARD-DEPENDS ON THE HASH-CONSED TermId, and porting it is the change's main risk: `classify_effect_label` (anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:2979-2990) tests `type_params.iter().any(|(_, tid)| *tid == t)` to recognise an effect-row param (`EffP`), by TermId identity — its comment cites WI-632 for choosing reference identity over a name-string match. Under `Var` this becomes `matches!(kb.get_term(t), Term::Var(v2) if *v2 == v)`: the same judgement (a var's identity is its VarId — CLAUDE.md's representation note: hash-consing is storage, not identity), but if it is missed the row param silently reclassifies `RowParam` -> `Unreadable` and the C++ signature degrades with no error. It must be ported WITH a driven cpp-gen test over an op declaring an effect-row param.\n\nWORK. (i) Change the field on `OpInfoRecord` (op_info.rs:52), `OpSignature` (op_info.rs:89) and `OperationInfoFull` (typing.rs:12965) to `Vec<(Symbol, Var)>`. (ii) Make `extract_type_params` total: map every element, and raise loudly on a non-`Term::Var(Global)` rather than dropping it. (iii) Port every reader listed above — replace each `kb.get_term(tid)` destructure with a direct `Var` read and each `TermIdView(tid)` re-wrap with `Value::Var(v)`. (iv) `typing.rs:10115` calls `walk_type_deep(kb, &subst, *var_term)`, which takes a TermId: either alloc the var term at the call site or route through the value walk — pick one and state which in a comment (the surrounding WI-394 code already surfaces a `Value::Node` binding via `surface_node_binding_to_term`, so do not regress that). (v) Decide whether `sort_type_params_as_pairs` (typing.rs:23392, `Rc<Vec<(Symbol, TermId)>>`) moves in the same change — `rigidify_op_type_params` consumes the two concatenated, so leaving them in different types forces a conversion at typing.rs:33204; moving both is the cheaper end state. (vi) Fold in the corresponding loud-vs-silent decision for `sort_type_params_as_pairs`' skip, or record explicitly why it stays a filter.\n\nACCEPTANCE: no reader destructures a `type_params` entry out of a `TermId`; a non-var entry is a loud error rather than a silent drop, with the comment stating that its reachability is defensive and unmeasured; the cpp-gen effect-row-param classification is covered by a driven test that fails if the identity port is wrong; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: design question raised 2026-07-28 — \"can `OperationInfoFull.type_params` be `Vec<(Symbol, Value)>` (or `TermView::Item`) and use a carrier-neutral interface for parsing?\". My first answer measured that the producer always writes `Value::Term` and concluded `Value` adds no inhabitant; that was true and beside the point — the field holds VARIABLES, and `Var` (not a carrier-neutral `Value`) is the honest type. This is NOT a carrier-neutrality change: a variable is not a carrier, and none is gained. What is gained is that the destructure-and-drop becomes unrepresentable. `extract_type_params`' use of the term-only `head_field_term` + `list_to_vec` (vs the carrier-neutral `head_field` + `value_list_to_vec` its siblings `effects_of_head` / `clause_list_field` / `extract_params` use) was measured to be a no-op difference today and is NOT in scope.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/{op_info.rs, typing.rs}, rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T13:53:32Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-849", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with TWO deviations from the ticket, both driven by measurement.\n\n(1) The ticket specified a LOUD PANIC for a non-var `type_params` entry, on the stated assumption that its reachability was 'unmeasured / defensive — the loader is the only in-tree producer'. MEASURED (2026-07-28): that is FALSE. A hand-written `fact OperationInfo(name: <any resolvable symbol>, return_type: Int64, ..., type_params: cons(head: 42, tail: nil()), meta: meta())` reaches the arm from ordinary user source and panicked the loader. Two earlier probe spellings did NOT reach it and would have 'confirmed' the ticket's assumption: `type_params: 42` (a non-list — `list_to_vec` yields no entries) and a well-formed cons list whose `name:` does not resolve to a `Term::Ref` (`head_name_ref` returns None, so `build_op_signatures` skips the fact). The decisive spelling needs BOTH a resolvable name AND a real cons list AND a `return_type`. So the fix is a TYPER DIAGNOSTIC, not a panic: `build_op_signatures` carries the offending entries out of the pass it already makes, and `type_check_sorts_collect` reports one `TypeError::Other` per entry. The decode cannot raise itself — it runs under `&KnowledgeBase` with no error channel, and pre-typer (const-purity gate, eq-dispatch build).\n\n(2) ROOT CAUSE of that reachability is NOT type-param-specific and is left OPEN. `fact mk(a: 1, undeclared_field: 2)` over `entity mk(a: Int64)` LOADS CLEAN — an UNDECLARED named arg on a fact head is accepted generally. `type_params` is not a declared field of the stdlib `OperationInfo` entity (stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill:641 lists name/params/return_type/effects/requires/ensures/meta and no type_params — the loader appends it directly at load.rs:13655), so the label is only writable BECAUSE of that hole. Closing it is what would make the malformed entry genuinely unreachable, and it is the WI-805/808/809 family's missing member (those cover tuple labels, entity fields and named ARGUMENT lists; a fact head's unknown label is not covered). Worth its own work item — deliberately not spun off here.\n\nSCOPE NOTE vs the ticket's step (v): `sort_type_params_as_pairs` was NOT moved to `Var`, on measurement rather than the ticket's guess that moving both is cheaper. Its consumers want the param as a TERM (typing.rs:20514 feeds `Value::term`, 10271 walks it through a view) and several run under `&KnowledgeBase` where they could not re-alloc; its own `Var::Global` filter is also load-bearing and documented (a param may alias a CONCRETE sort, `sort T = Int64`, which is not skolemizable — so its skip is intentional and stays). The two tables meet at exactly one place, the `rigidify_op_type_params` concat, where the sort side converts with a loud `unreachable!` licensed by that filter.\n\nCOVERAGE: the cpp-gen effect-row-param identity port (TermId compare -> Var compare, lib.rs `classify_effect_label`) is pinned by the EXISTING `effect_gate_test::effect_row_parameter_is_not_gated` + `stdlib_monad_...`; verified by deliberately breaking the identity test and watching BOTH fire ('declares an effect whose kind C++ codegen cannot read'). No new cpp-gen test was needed. New tests: 4 in wi849_type_param_var_test.rs, including a CONTROL that a hand-written OperationInfo with a well-formed empty type_params list still loads (without it, the refusal test would pass on an implementation that rejected hand-written OperationInfo facts wholesale).\n\nA SEVENTH reader the ticket did not enumerate was found BY THE COMPILER once the type changed: `op_tp_pinning_params` (typing.rs:5687), a third silent `filter_map` drop. That is the argument for the type change over enumerating the sites.\n\nFull workspace: 2720 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-28T13:53:21Z")
